# Content Radar — environment variables
#
# Copy to .env and fill in:  cp .env.example .env
# .env is gitignored and must never be committed.
#
# MVP NEEDS NONE OF THESE. Every MVP source (RSS, Hacker News Algolia, arXiv)
# and every MVP delivery channel (phone push, email, in-session file) is keyless.
# You only need this file when you add Telegram.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Telegram delivery  (optional — v1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The only channel that needs a secret, and the only two-way one: replying to
# the message is what later makes "reply 2 to pick topic 2" possible.
#
# Both values are free and take about five minutes to get:
#   1. Message @BotFather on Telegram, send /newbot, follow the prompts.
#      It replies with the bot token — that is TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN.
#   2. Send your new bot any message, then open
#      https://api.telegram.org/bot<YOUR_TOKEN>/getUpdates
#      and read result[0].message.chat.id — that is TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID.
#
# If either is unset, the Telegram channel reports itself unavailable and is
# skipped. It never breaks a run.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately absent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY is NOT needed. The editorial layer runs inside a Claude Code
# session via /radar rather than through the API, so there is no key to manage
# and no per-token cost on top of the subscription. If you find yourself adding
# this variable, check docs/project_spec.md first — it probably means editorial
# logic is drifting into Python, which is the one thing this design avoids.
